\begin{abstract}
Quantum kernel methods provide a convex optimization landscape for learning from quantum-native data, avoiding the barren plateau issues typical of variational quantum algorithms. However, recent theoretical findings reveal that global fidelity kernels suffer from exponential concentration, rendering their Gram matrices vulnerable to noise and finite sampling budgets. In this work, we present an empirical benchmark comparing global Fidelity Quantum Kernels (FQ) and 1-qubit Projected Quantum Kernels (PQK) against classical Radial Basis Function (RBF) support vector machines on the 1D Transverse Field Ising Model (TFIM) across its quantum phase transition. We demonstrate that while classical single-site observables fail to resolve the collective phase boundary (achieving $\sim 55\%$ accuracy with small training sets), quantum kernels achieve $95.0\%$ test accuracy with as few as 10 training samples. Furthermore, under single-qubit depolarizing noise up to $p=0.08$, the off-diagonal variance of the fidelity kernel drops by $73.7\%$, while PQK retains structural contrast with only a $6.8\%$ variance decay. These results validate theoretical predictions that local metric projections mitigate noise-induced Gram matrix flattening under finite sampling budgets.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Quantum Machine Learning, Projected Quantum Kernels, Transverse Field Ising Model, Exponential Concentration, Noise Robustness.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Quantum Machine Learning (QML) has emerged as a promising paradigm for discovering quantum order and classifying phases of many-body quantum states. While Parameterized Quantum Circuits (PQCs) often encounter barren plateaus where gradients vanish exponentially with system size, quantum kernel methods map data into high-dimensional Hilbert spaces where convex optimization guarantees convergence to a global optimum.

Despite this convexity, global fidelity kernels $K^{\text{FQ}}(\psi_i, \psi_j) = \vert{}\langle \psi_i \vert{} \psi_j \rangle\vert{}^2$ face severe limitations due to exponential concentration. As established by Thanasilp et al., high circuit expressivity, global observables, and hardware noise compress kernel values towards $1/2^n$, causing statistical estimates from finite measurement shots to collapse into data-independent Gram matrices. Huang et al. proposed Projected Quantum Kernels (PQK) using reduced physical density matrices as an alternative to preserve sample efficiency and avoid Hilbert space orthogonality.

In this paper, we empirically evaluate the sample efficiency, noise resilience, and finite-shot performance of FQ and PQK architectures on classifying ground states of the 1D Transverse Field Ising Model (TFIM) across its ferromagnetic-paramagnetic transition.

\section{Theoretical Framework}

\subsection{Physical System: 1D Transverse Field Ising Model}
We study a 1D chain of $n$ spin-$\frac{1}{2}$ particles governed by the Hamiltonian with periodic boundary conditions:
\begin{equation}
H = -J \sum_{i=1}^n Z_i Z_{i+1} - g \sum_{i=1}^n X_i
\end{equation}
where $J$ denotes the nearest-neighbor spin coupling, $g$ represents the transverse magnetic field, and $X_i, Z_i$ are Pauli matrices. The system exhibits a second-order quantum phase transition at the critical ratio $g/J = 1$, separating the ordered ferromagnetic phase ($g/J < 1$) from the disordered paramagnetic phase ($g/J > 1$).

\subsection{Quantum Kernel Formulations}
Given a collection of ground states $\{\vert{}\psi(g_i)\rangle\}_{i=1}^N$, we evaluate two distinct similarity metrics:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Fidelity Quantum Kernel (FQ):} Evaluates the full Hilbert-space inner product:
    \begin{equation}
    K_{ij}^{\text{FQ}} = |\langle \psi(g_i) | \psi(g_j) \rangle|^2
    \end{equation}
    \item \textbf{Projected Quantum Kernel (PQK):} Projects the global state onto local 1-qubit reduced density matrices $\rho_k(g_i) = \text{Tr}_{\bar{k}}[\vert{}\psi(g_i)\rangle \langle \psi(g_i)\vert{}]$:
    \begin{equation}
    K_{ij}^{\text{PQ}} = \exp\left(-\gamma \sum_{k=1}^n \|\rho_k(g_i) - \rho_k(g_j)\|_F^2\right)
    \end{equation}
    where $\Vert{}\cdot\Vert{}_F$ is the Frobenius norm and $\gamma > 0$ is a scale hyperparameter.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Experimental Methodology}
Ground states were computed via exact sparse diagonalization using SciPy for $n=8$ spins across 40 logarithmically spaced values of $g/J \in [0.1, 10.0]$. Ground states were labeled $y_i \in \{-1, +1\}$ corresponding to ferromagnetic and paramagnetic regimes.

Models were evaluated via 5-fold cross-validation across training subsets $N \in \{6, 10, 16, 24\}$ using stratified sampling. We compared:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Support Vector Classification (SVC) trained on precomputed $K^{\text{FQ}}$.
    \item SVC trained on precomputed $K^{\text{PQ}}$ ($\gamma = 1.0$).
    \item Classical RBF-SVC trained on single-site expectations $\langle Z_k \rangle = \text{Tr}(Z_k \rho_k)$.
\end{enumerate}

To assess noise stability, we applied single-qubit depolarizing noise $\mathcal{E}(\rho_k) = (1-p)\rho_k + p \frac{I}{2}$ for $p \in [0.00, 0.08]$, and simulated finite measurement budgets $S \in [50, 10000]$ shots per Gram matrix element.

\section{Results and Discussion}

\subsection{Sample Efficiency and Inductive Advantage}
As summarized in Table \ref{tab:sample_eff}, both quantum formulations demonstrate substantial sample efficiency over the classical baseline. At $N=6$, FQ and PQK achieve $92.5\%$ test accuracy, reaching $95.0\%$ at $N=16$. In contrast, classical RBF-SVC trained on 1-point expectations remains near chance ($\sim 52.5\% - 57.5\%$), as local expectations alone lack the multi-site correlation information necessary to pinpoint the phase boundary.

